.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":1310000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":19032,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":22401,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2601.42,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":201.01,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[{"title":"Annual governance statement 2026","url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38553/annual-governance-statement-2026.pdf","committee":"Audit & Governance","date":"14/07/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Audit & Governance","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"External Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-23","committee":"Joint Planning Policy Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-16","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-27","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"External Overview & Scrutiny","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Rough sleeper services funding faces cessation risk from 2026/27 with no continuation plan stated","lead":"A material service (\u00a3140k+) dependent entirely on time-limited government grants faces discontinuation unless funding is renewed; the council has not articulated either a continuation strategy or fallback funding source, creating financial and service\u2026","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Contract Waivers","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38563/38563.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Strong cash position now (\u00a335.3m) but projected to decline to \u00a318m within one year.","lead":"The declining cash position, combined with the structural deficit, shows the council is already in drawdown mode. At current deficit rates, reserves would be significantly depleted by the early 2030s, tightening liquidity and financial flexibility.","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"All PWLB borrowing repaid; zero long-term debt outstanding as at end of 2025/26.","lead":"While debt-free appears positive, for a council facing a growing \u00a37.265m revenue deficit, the absence of an established PWLB relationship or credit line could limit borrowing options if deficits accelerate. It also signals the council has not drawn on\u2026","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Audit & Governance","paper_title":"Contract Waivers","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38563/38563.pdf","headline":"Rough sleeper services funding faces cessation risk from 2026/27 with no continuation plan stated","detail":"The council is delivering rough sleeper services valued at approximately \u00a370,031 (RSI Initiative), \u00a325,125 (Winter Pressures Move On Worker), and \u00a345,472 (RSAP accommodation support) totalling \u00a3140,628 in 2025/26 through Encompass Southwest under procurement waivers. All three funding streams are temporary government grants from MHCLG. The council explicitly states it will review service provision only once clarity emerges from MHCLG on funding arrangements from 2026/27 onwards, indicating no contingency planning is currently underway.","category":"income","matters":"A material service (\u00a3140k+) dependent entirely on time-limited government grants faces discontinuation unless funding is renewed; the council has not articulated either a continuation strategy or fallback funding source, creating financial and service delivery uncertainty from 2027 onwards.","figures":"\u00a370,031 RSI services, \u00a325,125 Winter Pressures funding, \u00a345,472 RSAP revenue = \u00a3140,628 in 2025/26; RSI funded as extension to original 3-year allocation; all other streams ad hoc or temporary","quote":"Once there is some clarity from MHCLG over funding arrangements from 2026/27, we will review our services to rough sleepers.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Audit & Governance","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","headline":"Strong cash position now (\u00a335.3m) but projected to decline to \u00a318m within one year.","detail":"As at 31 March 2026, the council held \u00a323.4m in short-term investments plus \u00a311.9m in the current bank account. The council projects this will fall to circa \u00a318m by 31 March 2027. While expressing confidence in meeting obligations, this trajectory reflects ongoing reserve drawdown to fund operational deficits.","category":"reserves","matters":"The declining cash position, combined with the structural deficit, shows the council is already in drawdown mode. At current deficit rates, reserves would be significantly depleted by the early 2030s, tightening liquidity and financial flexibility.","figures":"\u00a335.3m total cash as at 31 March 2026 (\u00a323.4m short-term investments, \u00a311.9m current account); projected \u00a318m by 31 March 2027","quote":"The Council is predicting to have cash balances of circa \u00a318m (current bank account and short-term treasury deposits) as at the 31st March 2027 and is therefore confident it can meet its financial obligations for the foreseeable future.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Audit & Governance","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","headline":"All PWLB borrowing repaid; zero long-term debt outstanding as at end of 2025/26.","detail":"Torridge has repaid its final Public Works Loan Board loan, leaving zero long-term borrowing commitments. The report presents this as a positive, noting only a very small proportion of revenue budget goes to capital financing costs.","category":"borrowing","matters":"While debt-free appears positive, for a council facing a growing \u00a37.265m revenue deficit, the absence of an established PWLB relationship or credit line could limit borrowing options if deficits accelerate. It also signals the council has not drawn on long-term capital finance in recent years.","figures":"Zero long-term borrowing as at close of 2025/26; prior debts held with Public Works Loan Board","quote":"The balance of long-term borrowing commitments all held with the Public Works Loan Board at the close of the financial year 2026/27 was zero, the final loan having been repaid in the year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Audit & Governance","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","headline":"Temporary Accommodation demand flagged as uncontrolled and ongoing cost risk.","detail":"Temporary accommodation is explicitly identified as a financial risk that could increase further, alongside capital programme cost overruns and pay inflation. The paper provides no quantification of current spend or trajectory, indicating it is an emerging area of concern.","category":"demand","matters":"Temporary Accommodation is typically the fastest-growing and least controllable cost line for councils. Uncontrolled TA demand can rapidly deplete reserves and trigger unexpected budget pressures beyond forecasts.","figures":"No figures provided in this report","quote":"In particular the costs of delivering the capital programme could increase beyond the funding envelope, demand for Temporary Accommodation could increase further and pay and non-pay inflation could again return.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Audit & Governance","paper_title":"Going Concern Report - 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38554/38554.pdf","headline":"A \u00a37.265m cumulative funding gap over 2026/27\u20132030/31 with only \u00a32.001m in Budgetary Stabilisation Reserve to cover it.","detail":"The council's Medium Term Financial Strategy shows growing annual revenue deficits: \u00a30.399m in 2026/27 rising to \u00a31.932m by 2030/31. The Budgetary Stabilisation Reserve can cover only the first two years of deficits (\u00a31.760m), leaving a \u00a35.505m gap from 2028/29 onwards that must be closed through identified savings or new income. The paper states the council will \"continue to seek operational efficiencies and new income streams in order to bring the Council's finances back into balance and therefore avoid the need to drain the Budgetary Stabilisation Reserve.\"","category":"savings","matters":"A structural revenue deficit that cannot be funded by existing reserves without new decisions represents material solvency risk. Without identified solutions, the council exhausts its financial buffer and faces potential financial distress.","figures":"\u00a37.265m cumulative deficit; annual deficits of \u00a30.399m (2026/27), \u00a31.361m (2027/28), \u00a31.679m (2028/29), \u00a31.894m (2029/30), \u00a31.932m (2030/31); Budgetary Stabilisation Reserve \u00a32.001m as at 1st April 2026","quote":"There is therefore only enough resources in the Budgetary Stabilisation reserve to meet the cumulative deficit anticipated for the next two financial years.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-14"},"name":"Torridge","findings_total":46,"news_drafts_total":3}}